1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,462
The deadliest plane crash in history killed 583 people.

2
00:00:04,462 --> 00:00:07,673
And neither aircraft ever left the ground.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,108
Two Boeing seven forty-sevens. A fog bank moving at six metres a second.

4
00:00:13,108 --> 00:00:19,405
And one radio call that arrived, at the worst possible moment, as a three point seven second electronic squeal.

5
00:00:20,533 --> 00:00:28,732
The captain who caused it was his airline's symbol of safety — his face was in the ad in the in-flight magazine, on board the plane he crashed.

6
00:00:28,732 --> 00:00:33,124
When the airline heard the news, they tried to call him. To lead the investigation.

7
00:00:34,266 --> 00:00:45,107
This is the full story of Tenerife — the bomb that put both planes there, the thirteen seconds that killed 583 people, the myths everyone repeats, and what the truth actually was.

8
00:00:46,233 --> 00:00:49,629
Tenerife. The deadliest crash in aviation history.

9
00:00:50,766 --> 00:00:52,568
Chapter one. The bomb.

10
00:00:53,699 --> 00:00:54,813
March 27th, 1977.

11
00:00:54,813 --> 00:01:02,237
A small bomb planted by Canary Islands separatists explodes in a florist shop inside Las Palmas airport on Gran Canaria.

12
00:01:02,237 --> 00:01:07,805
Eight people are injured. Nobody dies. Then a phone call warns of a second bomb.

13
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:13,108
Las Palmas closes to search for a bomb that does not exist.

14
00:01:13,108 --> 00:01:20,066
Every inbound flight diverts to the only other runway in the islands: Los Rodeos, a small regional airport on Tenerife.

15
00:01:20,066 --> 00:01:24,241
Among them, two Boeing seven forty-sevens that were never supposed to meet.

16
00:01:25,366 --> 00:01:28,700
K L M Flight four eight zero five from Amsterdam.

17
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:32,034
Pan Am Flight one seven three six from Los Angeles.

18
00:01:32,034 --> 00:01:38,035
The Pan Am crew asked to circle and wait — they had the fuel. Spanish control said no.

19
00:01:38,035 --> 00:01:39,368
Land at Los Rodeos.

20
00:01:40,499 --> 00:01:42,928
Chapter two. The airport that couldn't see.

21
00:01:44,066 --> 00:01:50,995
Los Rodeos sits at 633 metres of elevation — high enough that clouds don't hover above the runway.

22
00:01:50,995 --> 00:01:52,534
They roll across it.

23
00:01:52,534 --> 00:01:57,153
Visibility that afternoon swung from 900 metres to under 100 in minutes.

24
00:01:58,299 --> 00:02:02,309
One runway. One taxiway — now a parking lot for diverted airliners.

25
00:02:02,309 --> 00:02:06,653
Which meant both seven forty-sevens had to taxi ON the active runway itself.

26
00:02:06,653 --> 00:02:10,329
The airport had no ground radar. The centerline lights were broken.

27
00:02:10,329 --> 00:02:14,338
Two controllers were working a day the airport was never built for.

28
00:02:15,466 --> 00:02:19,600
By the final minutes, the tower could not see either aircraft.

29
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:22,607
The two aircraft could not see each other.

30
00:02:22,607 --> 00:02:27,117
Three groups of professionals, all blind, connected by one congested radio frequency.

31
00:02:28,266 --> 00:02:31,427
Chapter three. The captain from the advertisement.

32
00:02:32,566 --> 00:02:40,133
The K L M captain was Jacob van Zanten — the airline's chief seven forty-seven instructor, the man who trained and certified their other pilots.

33
00:02:40,133 --> 00:02:42,856
His face ran in K L M's magazine ads.

34
00:02:42,856 --> 00:02:50,120
The copy of that magazine sitting in his passengers' seat pockets carried his photo, over the words: from the people who made punctuality possible.

35
00:02:51,266 --> 00:02:52,889
Punctuality was the trap.

36
00:02:52,889 --> 00:02:58,163
A new Dutch law made crew duty-time limits rigid — and criminally enforceable.

37
00:02:58,163 --> 00:03:01,814
Overrun the clock, and a captain could face prosecution.

38
00:03:01,814 --> 00:03:07,494
Van Zanten's window to fly home was closing, and hotels on Tenerife were full.

39
00:03:07,494 --> 00:03:11,145
250 passengers, nowhere to sleep, and a career-threatening deadline.

40
00:03:12,266 --> 00:03:19,329
So he made a decision that looked efficient: refuel now, at Los Rodeos, and skip the stop at Las Palmas.

41
00:03:19,329 --> 00:03:22,507
Fifty-five thousand five hundred litres — about 45 tonnes.

42
00:03:22,507 --> 00:03:27,804
It cost 35 extra minutes on the ground. The fog arrived in exactly that window.

43
00:03:27,804 --> 00:03:31,335
And every litre would soon be fuel for a fire.

44
00:03:32,466 --> 00:03:37,362
While the K L M tanked, the Pan Am crew walked over with a tape measure.

45
00:03:37,362 --> 00:03:41,646
Could they taxi around the parked jumbo and just leave? They measured the gap.

46
00:03:41,646 --> 00:03:43,176
Three point seven metres short.

47
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:48,830
The man in the safety advertisement. About to cause the deadliest crash in history.

48
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:50,448
Because being late was illegal.

49
00:03:50,448 --> 00:03:54,331
Remember that next time your boss says the deadline is the priority.

50
00:03:55,466 --> 00:03:57,660
Chapter four. Clipper Victor.

51
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:04,016
The Pan Am jet deserves a moment. Registration November seven three six Papa Alpha.

52
00:04:04,016 --> 00:04:11,468
In January 1970, this exact aircraft flew the first Boeing seven forty-seven passenger flight in history, New York to London.

53
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:19,208
Seven months later it was hijacked to Havana — where Fidel Castro personally came out to the runway to look the giant over.

54
00:04:19,208 --> 00:04:23,519
The most famous seven forty-seven in the world. It had one more appointment with history.

55
00:04:24,666 --> 00:04:26,939
Chapter five. Thirteen seconds.

56
00:04:28,066 --> 00:04:31,022
Here is the geometry of the next four minutes.

57
00:04:31,022 --> 00:04:36,933
The K L M taxis all the way down the runway and turns 180 degrees, ready for takeoff.

58
00:04:36,933 --> 00:04:44,814
The Pan Am follows behind it on the same runway, told to exit at the third taxiway and let the K L M go.

59
00:04:44,814 --> 00:04:48,755
In fog, with unreadable signs, past turns a jumbo can barely make.

60
00:04:49,899 --> 00:04:57,964
The exit they were given required a 148 degree double turn — a manoeuvre the pilots' association later called practically impossible for a seven forty-seven.

61
00:04:57,964 --> 00:05:03,447
So the Pan Am crew did the reasonable thing: they rolled on toward the next, gentler exit.

62
00:05:03,447 --> 00:05:04,737
Still on the runway.

63
00:05:05,866 --> 00:05:09,121
At the far end, van Zanten pushes the throttles up.

64
00:05:09,121 --> 00:05:13,677
His first officer — a man he personally trained and certified — stops him.

65
00:05:14,799 --> 00:05:18,031
Wait a minute. We do not have an A T C clearance.

66
00:05:19,166 --> 00:05:24,508
The tower reads back a route clearance — the flight path to fly after departure.

67
00:05:24,508 --> 00:05:29,494
It is not a takeoff clearance. But van Zanten is already releasing the brakes.

68
00:05:29,494 --> 00:05:33,768
His first officer, rushing the readback, ends it with four ambiguous words.

69
00:05:34,899 --> 00:05:36,299
We are now at takeoff.

70
00:05:37,433 --> 00:05:40,739
At takeoff. Taking off. Waiting at the takeoff point.

71
00:05:40,739 --> 00:05:45,883
The phrase means everything and nothing. The tower answers: okay. Stand by for takeoff.

72
00:05:45,883 --> 00:05:47,352
I will call you.

73
00:05:47,352 --> 00:05:54,700
And at that exact moment, the Pan Am crew, hearing the K L M's readback, keys their microphone in alarm.

74
00:05:55,833 --> 00:05:59,363
Two radios transmitting at once do not take turns. They collide.

75
00:05:59,363 --> 00:06:09,312
In the K L M cockpit, the tower's warning and the Pan Am's we're still taxiing down the runway arrived as one thing: a three point seven four second electronic squeal.

76
00:06:09,312 --> 00:06:11,559
The only word that survived was okay.

77
00:06:12,699 --> 00:06:16,774
One man on the K L M flight deck still felt it.

78
00:06:16,774 --> 00:06:21,528
The flight engineer, Willem Schreuder, asked the question that could have saved 583 lives.

79
00:06:22,666 --> 00:06:25,655
Is he not clear, then? That Pan American?

80
00:06:26,799 --> 00:06:33,304
Van Zanten — the chief instructor, the man from the advertisement — answered with two words: oh, yes.

81
00:06:33,304 --> 00:06:38,002
The engineer did not press. You did not press Mr. K L M.

82
00:06:38,002 --> 00:06:41,976
The jumbo was already accelerating through the fog at 140 knots.

83
00:06:43,099 --> 00:06:45,215
Chapter six. The collision.

84
00:06:46,333 --> 00:06:52,336
In the Pan Am cockpit, Captain Victor Grubbs saw landing lights emerge from the fog — coming at him.

85
00:06:52,336 --> 00:06:56,760
The C V R recorded him shouting: there he is — look at him.

86
00:06:56,760 --> 00:06:59,288
Goddamn, that son of a bitch is coming.

87
00:06:59,288 --> 00:07:02,764
He firewalled the throttles and swung the jumbo toward the grass.

88
00:07:03,899 --> 00:07:12,927
Van Zanten saw the Pan Am too late and hauled his aircraft into the air early — so hard the tail dragged 22 metres along the concrete in a shower of sparks.

89
00:07:12,927 --> 00:07:18,006
It almost worked. The nose gear cleared the Pan Am. The main gear and two engines did not.

90
00:07:19,133 --> 00:07:31,582
The K L M tore through the Pan Am's upper deck at 260 kilometres per hour, stayed airborne for 150 metres, stalled, and came down — 45 tonnes of fresh fuel igniting on impact.

91
00:07:31,582 --> 00:07:35,610
Everyone aboard the K L M died. All 248 of them.

92
00:07:35,610 --> 00:07:41,102
The fire burned so hot the aircraft was not fully identifiable until the next day.

93
00:07:42,233 --> 00:07:47,832
And in the fog, the fire crews fought that burning wreck believing they were fighting the whole disaster.

94
00:07:47,832 --> 00:07:52,499
They could not see that a second Boeing seven forty-seven was burning 300 metres away.

95
00:07:53,633 --> 00:07:55,514
Chapter seven. Sixty-one.

96
00:07:56,633 --> 00:08:04,296
On the Pan Am, 61 of 396 lived — every one of them from the forward left section, and every one of them saved themselves.

97
00:08:04,296 --> 00:08:10,426
First Officer Robert Bragg looked up for the fire handles and realized the entire top of the aeroplane was gone.

98
00:08:11,566 --> 00:08:15,711
Survivors jumped six metres from the wing onto grass and concrete.

99
00:08:15,711 --> 00:08:27,016
Purser Dorothy Kelly, thrown clear in the impact, went back — dragging passengers away, then finding Captain Grubbs dazed on the ground, pulling him clear moments before an engine exploded.

100
00:08:27,016 --> 00:08:29,277
She was later decorated for it.

101
00:08:30,399 --> 00:08:38,119
The rescue services never reached them in time to matter — everyone who escaped was already out before help found the second aircraft.

102
00:08:38,119 --> 00:08:48,187
And on the K L M side, exactly one name on the manifest survived: Robina van Lanschot, a tour guide who lived on Tenerife and simply chose not to reboard.

103
00:08:48,187 --> 00:08:50,201
She carried the guilt for decades.

104
00:08:51,333 --> 00:08:54,024
Chapter eight. The myths, tested.

105
00:08:55,166 --> 00:08:59,002
Myth one. The controllers were distracted, listening to a football match.

106
00:08:59,002 --> 00:09:12,952
Tested: sounds on the tower tape were interpreted by some listeners as a broadcast — but the claim was never substantiated, and even the Dutch investigators, who had every incentive to spread the blame, concluded it likely had no effect.

107
00:09:12,952 --> 00:09:16,090
Unproven. Repeat it as fact, and you're the misinformation.

108
00:09:17,233 --> 00:09:21,272
Verdict: unproven. And honestly — it wouldn't change a thing.

109
00:09:21,272 --> 00:09:23,696
No radar, no visibility, one radio.

110
00:09:23,696 --> 00:09:28,544
The system was blind long before anyone allegedly turned on a match.

111
00:09:29,666 --> 00:09:33,979
Myth two. The Pan Am crew caused it by missing their exit.

112
00:09:33,979 --> 00:09:45,481
Tested: the exit they were assigned required a 148 degree double turn that the pilots' association called practically impossible for a seven forty-seven — in fog, past broken lights and unreadable signs.

113
00:09:45,481 --> 00:09:49,075
Rolling to the next exit was the professional choice. False.

114
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:53,521
Myth three. A rogue cowboy captain took off without permission.

115
00:09:53,521 --> 00:10:04,149
Tested: van Zanten had a clearance — the wrong one — and twelve weeks of simulator instruction where the instructor grants his own clearances had primed him to hear what he expected.

116
00:10:04,149 --> 00:10:08,134
Add a criminal deadline and a closing fog window. Not a cowboy.

117
00:10:08,134 --> 00:10:13,449
A careful man inside a perfect trap. And that distinction is exactly why the fix worked.

118
00:10:14,566 --> 00:10:15,741
That's the real horror.

119
00:10:15,741 --> 00:10:21,615
Remove the villain, and what's left is a system that could do this to the best pilot the airline had.

120
00:10:21,615 --> 00:10:26,608
Fix the villain, you fix one man. Fix the system — you fix every flight after it.

121
00:10:27,733 --> 00:10:30,894
Chapter nine. What 583 lives bought.

122
00:10:32,033 --> 00:10:37,420
After Tenerife, the word takeoff was banned from radio except in the actual clearance itself.

123
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:39,935
Ambiguity became a violation. Readbacks became mandatory.

124
00:10:39,935 --> 00:10:46,400
The phrase line up and wait exists because we are now at takeoff meant two things at once.

125
00:10:47,533 --> 00:10:50,102
And the deeper fix: crew resource management.

126
00:10:50,102 --> 00:10:55,242
Meurs challenged his captain once and folded. Schreuder asked once and accepted oh yes.

127
00:10:55,242 --> 00:11:02,951
Today, every airline crew on Earth is trained — and required — to challenge the captain until the doubt is resolved.

128
00:11:02,951 --> 00:11:08,457
The most junior voice in the cockpit now carries veto power over the most senior.

129
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:14,265
583 people died on a runway in the fog without ever leaving the ground.

130
00:11:14,265 --> 00:11:18,931
In the 48 years since, runway collisions of that scale have killed exactly zero.

131
00:11:18,931 --> 00:11:20,597
That is what they bought.

132
00:11:21,733 --> 00:11:25,836
Tenerife happened because a warning turned into noise for less than four seconds.

133
00:11:25,836 --> 00:11:35,306
If that margin terrifies you, the story of the airliner that vanished for twelve years with 239 people aboard will keep you up tonight — it's on your screen now.

134
00:11:35,306 --> 00:11:36,884
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

